WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.706 --> 00:00:03.690
How similar are Japanese and Korean?

00:00:04.314 --> 00:00:05.323
Hold on.

00:00:06.504 --> 00:00:08.339
Okay, now we can start.

00:00:13.812 --> 00:00:14.821
Hello, everyone.

00:00:14.846 --> 00:00:17.953
Welcome to the Langfocus channel,
and my name is Paul.

00:00:17.978 --> 00:00:20.787
How similar are Japanese and Korean?

00:00:20.812 --> 00:00:22.771
That is the question of the day.

00:00:22.796 --> 00:00:25.997
Japan and Korea are geographically
very close to each other,

00:00:26.022 --> 00:00:28.901
and at first glance they
look like similar countries

00:00:28.926 --> 00:00:30.767
with similar cultures, in a way.

00:00:30.792 --> 00:00:34.549
But, just how similar are the
Japanese and Korean languages?

00:00:34.574 --> 00:00:37.510
Well, the short answer is
they're closer to each other

00:00:37.535 --> 00:00:39.334
than they are to any other languages,

00:00:39.359 --> 00:00:41.787
and they are remarkably
similar in some ways.

00:00:41.812 --> 00:00:44.016
But, in other ways,
they're also very different,

00:00:44.041 --> 00:00:46.670
and they are certainly not
mutually intelligible.

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:49.340
Disclaimer, I don't speak very much Korean,

00:00:49.365 --> 00:00:50.976
but I do speak Japanese,

00:00:51.001 --> 00:00:54.118
and I am aware of the differences
and similarities between them.

00:00:54.143 --> 00:00:56.762
Let's look at the similarities
in terms of grammar,

00:00:56.787 --> 00:00:59.826
vocabulary, pronunciation
and the writing systems.

00:00:59.851 --> 00:01:04.157
When it comes to syntax and grammar
Japanese and Korean are very similar.

00:01:04.182 --> 00:01:08.307
Japanese and Korean sentences can
often be translated word by word,

00:01:08.332 --> 00:01:11.926
with each word in one sentence
having an equivalent in the other.

00:01:11.951 --> 00:01:15.467
Let's look at a couple of Korean and
Japanese sentences side by side,

00:01:15.492 --> 00:01:17.078
and we can see the similarities.

00:01:17.103 --> 00:01:20.927
Again, I don't really speak much Korean,
so if my pronunciation is terrible,

00:01:20.952 --> 00:01:22.740
Koreans, please forgive me.

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:24.235
In English: I am a student.

00:01:24.664 --> 00:01:26.791
In Korean: na neun haksaeng-ida.

00:01:26.815 --> 00:01:29.323
In Japanese: watashi wa gakusei-desu.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:32.905
You can see that these sentences
are translatable word by word,

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:36.605
that each word in the first sentence has
an equivalent in the second sentence,

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:38.217
in the exact same position.

00:01:38.242 --> 00:01:40.124
So, they are directly translatable.

00:01:40.702 --> 00:01:43.686
One thing I want to point
out is the topic marker.

00:01:43.711 --> 00:01:46.352
That's the second word in
each of these sentences.

00:01:46.377 --> 00:01:49.673
The topic marker is something that's
kind of unique to Japanese and Korean.

00:01:49.698 --> 00:01:52.463
Topic marker points out
the topic of a sentence.

00:01:52.488 --> 00:01:54.502
That's different from
the grammatical subject,

00:01:54.527 --> 00:01:57.320
overarching focus are the
topic of the sentence.

00:01:57.345 --> 00:02:00.201
Another thing I want to point out
is the last word in each sentence.

00:02:00.226 --> 00:02:01.572
In Korean it's ida,

00:02:01.597 --> 00:02:03.677
and in Japanese it's desu.

00:02:03.702 --> 00:02:08.359
In both cases, that's like a copula,
like the word 'is' in English.

00:02:08.384 --> 00:02:10.184
So, in both Japanese and Korean,

00:02:10.209 --> 00:02:13.985
this is a standalone word that is placed
usually at the end of the sentence.

00:02:16.906 --> 00:02:19.830
In Korean: na neun mul eul masinda

00:02:20.172 --> 00:02:23.005
In Japanese: watashi wa mizu wo nomimasu

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:26.624
So, again we see that the topic marker
 is the second word in each sentence,

00:02:26.649 --> 00:02:31.386
but a more important thing that I want
to point out here is the SOV word order

00:02:31.411 --> 00:02:33.482
that means Subject-Object-Verb.

00:02:33.507 --> 00:02:37.840
So, we can see that the subject
is 'watashi' or 'na' in Korean,

00:02:37.865 --> 00:02:40.220
and then we have the object.

00:02:40.245 --> 00:02:41.562
The object is water.

00:02:41.587 --> 00:02:45.110
In Korean it's 'mul' and
in Japanese it's 'mizu',

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:47.330
and then we have the verb at the end.

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:50.952
So, in Korean 'masinda' and
in Japanese 'nomimasu'.

00:02:51.345 --> 00:02:53.361
But, before the verb,

00:02:53.386 --> 00:02:56.073
there is an interesting little word
that we don't have in English

00:02:56.098 --> 00:02:57.969
and that is an object marker.

00:02:57.994 --> 00:03:01.929
That's another unique thing that we
find in both Japanese and Korean

00:03:01.954 --> 00:03:05.821
used in exactly the same way, in exactly
the same position in the sentence.

00:03:05.846 --> 00:03:09.059
Even though, the grammar and sentence
structure of the two languages

00:03:09.084 --> 00:03:12.417
is remarkably similar,
the vocabulary is remarkably different.

00:03:12.442 --> 00:03:16.079
If a Japanese speaker heard Korean
without any prior exposure,

00:03:16.104 --> 00:03:18.904
they probably wouldn't understand
any of the words at all,

00:03:18.929 --> 00:03:21.020
or maybe just a word here and there.

00:03:21.045 --> 00:03:23.298
But, that doesn't mean that
those words aren't related.

00:03:23.368 --> 00:03:28.335
Korean and Japanese both inherited a large
number of loan words from Chinese,

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:32.914
approximately 60% of the vocabulary
in either language comes from Chinese.

00:03:33.017 --> 00:03:35.935
If there are so many Chinese
loan words in both languages,

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:38.362
then why are they not
mutually comprehensible?

00:03:38.446 --> 00:03:40.845
Well, it's important to note
that for both languages,

00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:43.721
in daily conversation, in informal speech,

00:03:43.746 --> 00:03:47.727
the Chinese loan words are not used
nearly as much as they are in writing.

00:03:47.752 --> 00:03:51.110
Also, the Chinese loan words are
different in either language.

00:03:51.135 --> 00:03:54.090
They entered the languages in
the form of Chinese characters,

00:03:54.115 --> 00:03:57.060
but the pronunciation of those
characters became adapted

00:03:57.093 --> 00:04:00.172
to fit the phonological
system of either languages.

00:04:00.418 --> 00:04:02.148
So, let's look at some examples.

00:04:02.173 --> 00:04:05.980
The first example is just the name of
those Chinese characters themselves.

00:04:06.005 --> 00:04:07.550
In Chinese: han zi

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:09.281
In Korean: hanja

00:04:09.306 --> 00:04:11.304
in Japanese: kanji

00:04:11.593 --> 00:04:15.077
So, you can see that the
pronunciation started as han zi,

00:04:15.102 --> 00:04:18.709
but it diverged and became
different in Korean and Japanese,

00:04:18.709 --> 00:04:21.008
even though it's the same two characters.

00:04:21.296 --> 00:04:22.413
Some other examples.

00:04:22.438 --> 00:04:24.127
In Korean: sajin.

00:04:24.152 --> 00:04:25.833
in Japanese: sashin.

00:04:25.858 --> 00:04:26.858
Photograph.

00:04:26.883 --> 00:04:28.359
The word for singer.

00:04:28.384 --> 00:04:29.906
In Korean: kasu

00:04:29.931 --> 00:04:31.756
In Japanese: kashu

00:04:32.166 --> 00:04:33.517
Word for promise

00:04:33.658 --> 00:04:35.483
In Korean: yagsog

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:37.706
In Japanese: yakusoku

00:04:37.793 --> 00:04:39.580
Those examples sound quite similar,

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:41.671
but sometimes they sound
really quite different.

00:04:41.696 --> 00:04:45.376
But, if we look at the romanized words,
sometimes we can see the similarities.

00:04:45.868 --> 00:04:47.536
This is the word for telephone.

00:04:47.561 --> 00:04:49.450
In Korean: jeonhwa.

00:04:49.633 --> 00:04:51.432
In Japanese: denwa.

00:04:51.743 --> 00:04:53.539
So, those come from the same characters,

00:04:53.564 --> 00:04:56.333
but, again, they're rendered
differently in pronunciation.

00:04:56.799 --> 00:04:58.474
The next word is school.

00:04:58.499 --> 00:05:00.021
In Korean: haggyou

00:05:00.137 --> 00:05:01.879
In Japanese: gakkou

00:05:02.596 --> 00:05:05.158
Another example is University.

00:05:05.183 --> 00:05:06.438
In Korean: daehag

00:05:06.856 --> 00:05:08.412
In Japanese: daigaku

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:09.773
Tofu.

00:05:09.798 --> 00:05:11.534
In Korean: dubu

00:05:11.559 --> 00:05:13.115
In Japanese: toufu

00:05:13.463 --> 00:05:15.502
The word for spa or hotspring.

00:05:15.527 --> 00:05:16.939
In Korean: oncheon

00:05:16.964 --> 00:05:18.376
In Japanese: onsen

00:05:18.954 --> 00:05:21.653
So, you can see that sometimes
the words are clearly related,

00:05:21.678 --> 00:05:24.266
but when you're listening to
the language being spoken,

00:05:24.291 --> 00:05:26.655
you probably wouldn't be
able to catch those words.

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:28.242
Chinese loan words aside,

00:05:28.267 --> 00:05:31.891
there are also some Japanese loan words
that have entered the Korean language

00:05:31.916 --> 00:05:34.281
The word for cheers, when
you're having a drink.

00:05:34.306 --> 00:05:36.057
In Korean: geongbae

00:05:36.082 --> 00:05:37.868
In Japanese: kanpai

00:05:38.003 --> 00:05:39.622
The Japanese word for bag.

00:05:40.512 --> 00:05:42.345
In Korean it's 'gabang'.

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:44.321
In Japanese it's 'kaban'.

00:05:44.346 --> 00:05:48.153
There are also a lot of words that are
native to both Korean and Japanese,

00:05:48.178 --> 00:05:49.854
that might be related to each other

00:05:49.879 --> 00:05:52.283
The word for island, in Korean: seom.

00:05:52.696 --> 00:05:54.434
In Japanese: shima.

00:05:54.459 --> 00:05:56.988
The word for village, in Korean: maeul

00:05:57.013 --> 00:05:58.846
In Japanese: mura.

00:05:58.871 --> 00:06:01.940
There are a lot of words like that that
I don't really think are connected,

00:06:01.965 --> 00:06:04.234
but linguists think might be connected.

00:06:04.259 --> 00:06:06.941
Interestingly though,
some of the grammatical particles

00:06:06.966 --> 00:06:09.749
have very similar pronunciations
in the two languages.

00:06:09.774 --> 00:06:11.559
For example, the word for above.

00:06:11.584 --> 00:06:13.203
In Korean: wiei.

00:06:13.369 --> 00:06:14.853
In Japanese: ue.

00:06:15.005 --> 00:06:16.099
Question particle.

00:06:16.163 --> 00:06:19.424
'Kka', in Korean double consonant,
 double k.

00:06:19.456 --> 00:06:21.195
In Japanese, it's 'ka'.

00:06:21.305 --> 00:06:23.090
The word for 'to' or 'towards'.

00:06:23.115 --> 00:06:25.734
In Korean, it's 'e'. In Japanese, it's 'e'.

00:06:25.759 --> 00:06:28.876
The subject marker,
different from the topic marker.

00:06:29.013 --> 00:06:32.649
Subject marker in Korean is 'ga' or 'i'.

00:06:32.674 --> 00:06:34.495
In Japanese it's just 'ga'.

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:38.891
So basically, Korean and Japanese have a
lot of vocabulary that are related,

00:06:38.916 --> 00:06:40.201
but not the same.

00:06:40.226 --> 00:06:43.761
It's probably enough to help you
remember that word if you study it,

00:06:43.786 --> 00:06:46.702
but not necessarily enough
to pick up that word

00:06:46.727 --> 00:06:48.772
 when you're hearing it in conversation.

00:06:48.797 --> 00:06:52.294
And, of course, most of the spoken
vocabulary seems to be unrelated

00:06:52.319 --> 00:06:55.739
or they're words that have very
ancient connections to each other.

00:06:55.764 --> 00:06:59.186
Now, let's look at the different
pronunciation of the two languages

00:06:59.211 --> 00:07:00.891
In some ways, they're similar.

00:07:00.922 --> 00:07:03.942
The basic phonetic sounds of the
two languages are quite similar,

00:07:03.967 --> 00:07:06.006
but the way those phonetic
sounds are arranged

00:07:06.031 --> 00:07:08.854
to create syllables is different
in the two languages.

00:07:08.879 --> 00:07:12.935
In Japanese, basically every
syllable has to end in a vowel.

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:16.377
You cannot have a word that ends
in a solid consonant like 'cut'.

00:07:16.402 --> 00:07:19.695
It has to be something like 'cutto'
with a vowel added to the end.

00:07:19.798 --> 00:07:23.392
But, in Korean, you can have a
consonant at the end of a syllable.

00:07:23.417 --> 00:07:26.250
That's why in one of the words
that we compared before,

00:07:26.275 --> 00:07:29.170
the word for promise, in
Japanese it's 'yakusoku'.

00:07:29.195 --> 00:07:31.202
You need those vowels after the consonants,

00:07:31.227 --> 00:07:33.258
but in Korean it's 'yagsogg'.

00:07:33.378 --> 00:07:36.829
Another difference is that in Japanese,
the pronunciation of consonants

00:07:36.854 --> 00:07:39.616
doesn't change depending on
their position in the word,

00:07:39.641 --> 00:07:41.275
they just are always the same.

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:43.871
But in Korean, there are
consonants that can change,

00:07:43.896 --> 00:07:46.469
depending on whether they're
at the beginning of the word,

00:07:46.469 --> 00:07:48.897
in the middle of the word,
or at the end of the word.

00:07:48.929 --> 00:07:52.745
Korean also has a greater variety
of vowels and diphthongs.

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:57.863
So, in general Korean pronunciation is just
more complex than Japanese pronunciation.

00:07:57.888 --> 00:08:00.975
One thing that is completely
different between the two languages

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:02.419
is their writing systems.

00:08:02.444 --> 00:08:04.940
Japanese is written using a combination

00:08:04.964 --> 00:08:07.931
of Chinese characters and two syllabaries.

00:08:07.956 --> 00:08:11.755
Those syllabaries consist of characters
that represent entire syllables,

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:14.518
rather than just individual
phonetic sounds.

00:08:14.543 --> 00:08:16.764
The Japanese writing
system is quite complex,

00:08:16.789 --> 00:08:21.376
and since you need to learn more than 2,000
kanji to be able to read at an adult level,

00:08:21.401 --> 00:08:23.835
it can take quite a lot of
time and efforts to learn.

00:08:23.878 --> 00:08:26.893
Korean on the other hand is
written in the Hangul alphabet,

00:08:26.918 --> 00:08:29.824
which is one of the simplest
writing systems in the world.

00:08:29.849 --> 00:08:32.317
Korean used to use hanja
or Chinese characters

00:08:32.342 --> 00:08:37.101
in the same way that Japanese uses kanji
in combination with hiragana and katakana,

00:08:37.126 --> 00:08:38.348
but they've done away with that.

00:08:38.373 --> 00:08:41.433
Now, they're just using the
simplified writing system of Hangul.

00:08:41.458 --> 00:08:44.569
The Hangul alphabet consists of
individual phonetic symbols,

00:08:44.594 --> 00:08:47.173
that are arranged together
to create syllables.

00:08:47.347 --> 00:08:49.387
In this example for Hanja.

00:08:49.412 --> 00:08:51.839
This is the sound for ha like an h

00:08:51.864 --> 00:08:53.880
 this is the sound for a

00:08:53.905 --> 00:08:55.620
this is the sound for n like an n,

00:08:55.620 --> 00:08:58.920
this is the sound for j or t, 
and this is a.

00:08:58.945 --> 00:09:04.586
So, where as Japanese requires you to learn
92 syllabaries symbols and 2,000 kanji.

00:09:04.611 --> 00:09:08.245
In Korean, you just have to
learn 24 phonetic symbols

00:09:08.270 --> 00:09:11.357
and you can use those to represent
any new words that you learn.

00:09:11.382 --> 00:09:14.992
So, to summarize Korean and
Japanese are very similar

00:09:15.017 --> 00:09:16.934
in terms of sentence structure and grammar,

00:09:16.959 --> 00:09:19.961
and they both have a large
number of Chinese loan words.

00:09:19.986 --> 00:09:23.860
But, the native vocabularies are quite
different as in the pronunciation,

00:09:23.885 --> 00:09:27.280
which makes these two languages
mutually unintelligible.

00:09:27.305 --> 00:09:30.439
On top of that, the two writing systems
are now completely different,

00:09:30.464 --> 00:09:35.216
with the new modern Korean system completely
avoiding Chinese characters these days.

00:09:35.287 --> 00:09:39.985
My feeling is that Japanese and Korean
did originate from the same language

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:41.045
a very long time ago,

00:09:41.070 --> 00:09:44.714
but they've been diverging for a
long time perhaps about 2,000 years,

00:09:44.739 --> 00:09:48.617
 so that now they're very different even
though structurally they're quite similar.

00:09:48.642 --> 00:09:50.497
Thank you for watching
the Langfocus channel.

00:09:50.522 --> 00:09:51.893
Leave your comments down below,

00:09:51.918 --> 00:09:54.750
especially if you have
studied Japanese or Korean

00:09:54.775 --> 00:09:57.917
and you can let us know about some
other similarities or differences

00:09:57.942 --> 00:09:59.474
that you have discovered.

00:09:59.499 --> 00:10:02.149
Thank you for watching and have a nice day.

